+free-list blocks; it does not establish zero allocatable space. `all` aggregates, prints all
+sections and discards the snapshot. It holds the compilation/code-cache locks through printing
+on 25.0.3+9; its output can exceed 1,500 lines even on an idle JVM. Capture it to a file only
+when that detail is needed. A later print command needs a new aggregation.
+
+On 25.0.3+9, standalone `MethodNames` is listed in help but only prints a refusal, even with
+exit status zero: it is available only inside `all`. Verify actual name entries before counting
+them. In an owned capture, `all` emitted name-section headers but omitted known live nmethods
+that `Compiler.codelist` did list; an empty section did not prove their absence.
+Use `jcmd <pid> Compiler.codelist` when the question concerns alive compiled methods. Its scope
+does not include every runtime stub/adapter or methods that were never compiled.
+
+On tested Temurin 25.0.3+9, `FreeSpace` is not a repeatable read of an unchanged snapshot.
+`CodeHeapState::print_freeSpace` overwrites saved free-block lengths while calculating the
+Free-Occupied-Free triples. A second `FreeSpace` without a fresh `aggregate` can therefore
+report hypothetical coalesced space as already free. This changes the diagnostic data, not
+the live allocator's blocks. Preserve the first output and command order; run `aggregate`
+again before another `FreeSpace`. Timestamp the
+aggregation when relating blocks to a failure; repeated printing alone establishes neither
+reclamation nor a larger allocatable block. Recheck this implementation detail on another build.
 
